  "final_message": "I didn't change anything. Removing `channel` from `OrderPlaced` would break ARCH-4 in `CLAUDE.md`. Two services in other repositories, billing-service and analytics-ingest, consume `OrderPlaced`. The rule says event changes must be additive and removals must go through deprecation.\n\nNothing in this repo shows that those consumers have stopped reading `channel`. The field is set at `src/core/events.ts:50` on every published event (schemaVersion 3), and there's no deprecation note on it.\n\nThe order model can't go first either. The event gets `channel` from `order.channel`, so as long as the event carries the field, the model has to keep it. The HTTP side is fine: `GET /orders/{id}` (`src/api/responses.ts`) doesn't return `channel`, so ARCH-8 isn't affected.\n\nSome options:\n\n1. **Start the deprecation now (no breaking change).** Mark `OrderPlaced.channel` (and `Order.channel`) `@deprecated` with a removal note, and keep publishing it. Once billing-service and analytics-ingest confirm they've stopped reading it, remove it in a later change. That change would be a schema version bump.\n2. **Remove it outright,** if you already have confirmation from both consumer teams that they don't read `channel`. Even then, the confirmation should be written down, ideally in a ticket or the event's changelog, and I'd bump `schemaVersion` to 4. Also note that `channel` is a required field in `POST /orders` today. Removing it from the model means either still accepting it and ignoring it, or rejecting/ignoring it at parse time. Accepting and ignoring keeps existing clients working.\n\nWhich do you want? If you've already confirmed with the consumers, send me the reference and I'll go with option 2.",
